          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "n "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Mark the position of two ends of the needle.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.125,
            0.116,
            0.502,
            0.178
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Now move the needle to a new position such that its south pole occupies the position previously occupied by its north pole.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.124,
            0.179,
            0.502,
            0.226
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In this way, proceed step by step till you reach the south pole of the magnet as shown in Fig. 12.3.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.125,
            0.227,
            0.502,
            0.273
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Join the points marked on the paper by a smooth curve. This curve represents a field line.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.124,
            0.274,
            0.502,
            0.368
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Repeat the above procedure and draw as many lines as you can. You will get a pattern shown in Fig. 12.4. These lines represent the magnetic field around the magnet. These are known as magnetic field lines.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.124,
            0.369,
            0.502,
            0.432
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Observe the deflection in the compass needle as you move it along a field line. The deflection increases as the needle is moved towards the poles.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.6,
            0.086,
            0.858,
            0.201
          ],
          "content": [
            {
